    "body": "Since 2025, U.S. biopharma companies have raised $37.2 billion—68% of total worldwide venture capital funding—with startups in the San Francisco Bay Area and Boston leading the way.\nBiopharma has collected $54.5 billion across 959 rounds globally since the start of 2025 through 2026’s third quarter, with U.S. companies attracting $37.2 billion—about 68% of the total funding.\nIn the U.S., the two leading biopharma hubs are the San Francisco Bay Area and Boston, raising $12.8 billion and $10.2 billion since 2025, respectively, according to an October biopharma report from J.P. Morgan. Together, the two areas gathered about 62% of all U.S. biopharma funding over the past seven quarters, “underscoring the continued concentration of capital in established life-sciences ecosystems,” according to the report.\nSan Diego, Philadelphia and the Research Triangle in North Carolina stood out as three other strong U.S. fundraisers, raising a collective $5.2 billion over the same time period.\nOutside of the U.S., European biopharma companies raised $9.2 billion from 2025 to 2026’s Q3, accounting for just under 17% of all capital deployed. In Europe, the U.K. raised the largest share, securing $3.1 billion across 58 rounds. Switzerland followed at $1.8 billion, with France drawing in $851 million and Germany raising $832 million.\nWorldwide, money deployed in the most recent quarter stayed concentrated in fewer, larger rounds. Half of the $1.1 billion in capital deployed came from just five of the 32 rounds.\nOverall, 2026 is on track to surpass last year’s total of $30.5 billion, with J.P. Morgan projecting full-year biopharma funding to reach about $33 billion.\nIPO\nBiotech IPOs raise $7.5B as sector’s performance rally flattens\nWhile biopharma financing exit activity has remained strong through the third quarter, public-market performance for the same period was muted, with most of the yearly gains for the U.S. biotech-specific index largely driven by the first half of 2026.\nOctober 26, 2026\n·\n1 min read\n·\nGabrielle Masson\nRead more",
